Verdict: bug found

Line 24: `(void)p; ck(pthread_mutex_lock(&m)); int must_wait=jobs==0; ck(pthread_mutex_unlock(&m));`
Line 26: `if(must_wait) ck(pthread_cond_wait(&cv,&m));`

The decision to wait is saved across an unlock/relock gap. Even though each individual access uses m, the signal can happen before registration and the cached predicate is stale.

| Step | Thread A | Thread B | Shared state |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | read jobs -> 0 under m |  | A caches must_wait=true |
| 2 | unlock(m) |  | gap before wait |
| 3 |  | jobs=1 under m | job queued |
| 4 |  | signal(cv) | A not yet waiting |
| 5 | lock(m) succeeds |  | A still uses old decision |
| 6 | cond_wait releases m and waits |  | no later signal; job exists |

Fix:
```diff
--- prog.c
+++ prog.c (fixed)
@@ -21,9 +21,7 @@
 static pthread_cond_t cv=PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER;
 static int jobs;
 static void *a(void *p) {
-    (void)p; ck(pthread_mutex_lock(&m)); int must_wait=jobs==0; ck(pthread_mutex_unlock(&m));
-    ck(pthread_mutex_lock(&m));
-    if(must_wait) ck(pthread_cond_wait(&cv,&m));
+    (void)p; ck(pthread_mutex_lock(&m)); while(jobs==0) ck(pthread_cond_wait(&cv,&m));
     assert(jobs==1); ck(pthread_mutex_unlock(&m)); return NULL;
 }
 static void *b(void *p) {
```

Testing jobs in a while loop without the gap lets cond_wait perform the atomic release-and-wait transition. Every wakeup uses the current predicate.

